.TH DATAPUMP-GUI 1 "November 2026" "Data Pump 001_00" "User Commands"
.nh
.ad l
.nr HY 0
.ds AD l
.SH NAME
datapump-gui \- graphical audio modem for text and files
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B datapump-gui
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B datapump-gui
sends and receives text, exact bit strings and files through an audio interface.
It provides a message editor, file attachments, clipboard actions, QR previews
and live signal plots.
.PP
An ordinary launch opens Fast Modem and listens on the selected audio device.
The Modem menu also offers Robust Modem and Legacy Modem.
Robust links one native C++ three-carrier DATA/Helper/complete-receiver stack
into the application; Python is not part of its operating path. Legacy supports
BPSK31, BPSK125 and Olivia-4/2k text.
The modes have separate settings.
Peers must select matching waveform, source, coding and key settings.
Live Robust also requires a shared public calendar and initial waveform ordinal,
plus a declared local input-noise model in Robust setup. Unknown-start
acquisition, native real-time capacity and physical-device qualification remain
unqualified. Robust audio CLI commands remain retired.
.PP
Robust keeps Baseband Osc, Shift Osc and Margin beside its main Carrier and
Shift frequency controls. Shift 0 disables Shift Osc as N/A and excludes all
Shift-model frequency and phase error. For a positive Shift, the Baseband clock
option shares the selected Baseband model between sampling and mixers, counted
once. Numerical oscillator/search information is under the Link planner's
hideable Model limits and references.
.PP
Fast Modem's Simulation page offers Live audio (the default), Simulation,
and Live loopback test. Ordinary Live uses the selected audio device and
does not insert a synthetic channel.
Simulation starts a local Fast transmission through the native Fast sampled
channel and receiver; results are labeled as simulated. Its controls set
assumed transmit power in watts, path loss in dB, receiver noise density in
dBm/Hz or Off, Baseband and Shift oscillator presets (including Off and Same
Baseband reference), Doppler/delay spread, additional selective AM, search
margin, and Shift frequency in Hz. These values describe the software model,
not measurements or physical radio controls. Shift frequency affects the
oscillator model only and does not change the Fast audio carrier or passband.
Simulation preserves channel tails and a scored quiet completion interval
without opening hardware audio.
.PP
Run loopback test sends a fixed short diagnostic through the selected channel
model, physical audio output, connected loopback path, and captured Fast
receiver. It uses the selected profile, device, output routing and encryption;
encrypted tests require the selected key. Capture starts before playback,
using the normal shared-audio handoff. Output gain is fixed at 0.1; the test
allows at most 75 seconds of audio and 110 seconds of elapsed time. The Console
message and file drafts are preserved and are not sent. Test status, counters
and captured receiver plots are labeled LOOPBACK TEST. No diagnostic enters
Signals history or creates a text/file or Save handle, and the test does not
start automatic listening. A pass requires exact diagnostic reception,
integrity verification and scored physical absence. Cancellation, limits and
audio discontinuities leave the test incomplete. A cable test exercises the
local audio path with modeled effects; it does not qualify RF propagation or
a named transceiver.
.PP
FLTK and Rev builds provide the same application controls.
The backend is selected at build time and reported by
.BR \-\-version ;
it cannot be switched at runtime.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print a brief usage summary and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and compiled GUI backend, then exit.
.TP
.B \-\-color
Use color where supported; this is the default.
.TP
.B \-\-monochrome
Use grayscale.
The last color or monochrome option wins.
.TP
.B \-\-simulation
Open Robust Modem with a continuous simulated noisy channel instead of live
audio. Emitted samples pass through the selected continuous sampled channel
and complete native receiver; plots show actual observations and score records.
.TP
.BI \-\-hardware-channel-test " yes|no"
Deprecated compatibility spelling; accepted but ignored and no longer exported.
Selected Doppler/delay spread and additional amplitude modulation apply in both
simulation and physical output. Defaults are None and Off. Disable effects using
those channel controls, not this old flag. Physical output adds no synthetic
noise, oscillator error or path loss. Enable Live/Duplex for a same-instance
speaker/microphone or cable loopback. Use \fB\-\-verbose\fP or
\fB\-\-verbose-file\fP to record actual channel parameters.
.TP
.B \-\-debug, \-\-debug-live-duplex
Run a bounded real-audio Robust GUI duplex diagnostic. Sends one raw 1 bit
through the ordinary transmit path, requires an explicit synthetic
\fB\-\-keyfile\fP and \fB\-\-tx-key named\fP, and enables Live/Duplex.
Requires a physical cable or acoustic path. Cannot combine with Simulation,
smoke or self-check. Reports JSON lines to stderr and closes on error,
physical reception or deadline. TX EOF alone or preexisting receive rows cannot pass.
Exit 0 means successful requested coverage; exit 2 means completed duplex
reception with unavailable requested UTC playback; exit 1 means failure.
Controller status and preparation errors are included in the log.
.TP
.BI \-\-debug-timeout " SECONDS"
Hardware diagnostic deadline, 10 through 300 seconds; default 120.
A stuck startup/shutdown watchdog exits 124 after a further 15 seconds, even
when stderr is blocked (a final log line is not guaranteed).
Timeout is incomplete coverage. Diagnostic closure during TX aborts immediately
and forfeits echo protection. These flags are not saved modem settings.
.SS Robust link settings
Supplying any link setting opens Robust Modem.
These options also accept
.BR \-\-option=value .
Unspecified settings retain their startup defaults.
Link power, path loss and noise describe the planner and simulated channel;
they do not set a radio's RF output power.
.TP
.BI \-\-keyset " amateur|none"
Select the built-in public amateur keyset or unload all keys. Cannot be combined
with \-\-keyfile; \-\-keyset none also cannot be combined with \-\-key-name or
\-\-tx-key named. With the amateur keyset, \-\-tx-key none disables transmission
encryption and selects plaintext reception while retaining loaded keys for
later selection. Omitting the option preserves the current key bank.
.TP
.BI \-\-calendar\-epoch " UTC|none"
Shared public calendar anchor in whole UTC seconds, 6 through 4294967289.
Default none leaves live operation unconfigured; simulation uses its own
agreed local calendar. Both peers must use the same anchor.
.TP
.BI \-\-calendar\-ordinal " N"
Shared unsigned initial waveform ordinal; default 0. It advances through DATA
and quiet slots and does not reset at message boundaries.
.TP
.BI \-\-live\-noise\-dbfs\-hz " DENSITY|unspecified"
Declared one-sided local input-noise density in normalized PCM squared per Hz,
from \-250 to 0 dBFS/Hz. Default unspecified leaves live reception unconfigured.
This is separate from simulated link noise in dBm/Hz and does not calibrate a device.
.TP
.BI \-\-fec " off|rs20|rs60"
Configured native short/long text error correction; default off. Peers must agree.
Short RS modes retain their CRC profile. Long text uses fixed 128-byte codewords:
Off, RS(128,106), or RS(128,80), each preceded by a 192-bit marker. Keyed long
words include a 32-byte interval tag. Decoding still requires two fully scored
quiet slots. CRC is not sender authentication; public AMATEUR keys do not supply
sender authentication. Exact raw-bit transmission bypasses text coding and FEC.
.TP
.BI \-\-helper\-power " POWER"
Requested Helper transmit power, separate from DATA power. Zero disables Helper.
Accepts power units such as W or mW; it does not set physical radio power.
.TP
.BI \-\-helper\-ratio\-limit " DB"
Maximum Helper-to-DATA power ratio: 10, 20, 40, 60, 80 or 90 dB.
The native allocation limits Helper power to the selected ratio.
.TP
.BI \-\-helper\-callsign " TEXT"
Up to 16 printable ASCII characters in keyed Helper metadata.
.TP
.BI \-\-tx\-dbm " DBM"
Transmit power for the link budget, from \-200 to 100 dBm.
Default: 3.
.TP
.BI \-\-path\-loss\-db " DB"
Path loss, from 0 to 500 dB.
Default: 120.
.TP
.BI \-\-noise\-dbm\-hz " DENSITY"
Receiver noise density, from \-250 to 0 dBm/Hz.
Default: \-164.
.TP
.BI \-\-oscillator " ID"
Baseband oscillator model for bounded receiver search and simulated impairments:
.BR crystal " (default), " gpsdo-xo ,
.BR gpsdo-tcxo " or " gpsdo-ocxo .
.TP
.BI \-\-rf\-oscillator " ID"
Shift oscillator model; the same choices plus cb-ssb (combined CB/ATS example)
and ic-7100, default crystal. Inactive at Shift 0. The RF-only examples do not
specify an audio clock and cannot be used as a shared Baseband clock.
The IC-7100 RF example does not specify the USB audio clock.
For a legacy positive-Shift shared reference, this flag selects the shared model.
.TP
.BI \-\-reference " independent|shared-radio"
Independent Baseband and Shift references (default), or one shared sampling/mixer
reference for a positive Shift. Shift 0 always uses only the Baseband model.
.B \-\-lf\-reference 0
is a legacy shared-reference shorthand, unrelated to Shift 0. GUI load maps an
active shared model into Baseband Osc and selects Shift Osc's Baseband clock
option. Independent UTC accuracy, region and offset controls do not change that topology.
.TP
.BI \-\-clock\-sync " SPEC"
Default retains the broad arrival-time search around the configured public
calendar. An explicit example is
GPS_1ms-400ms_region-2564ms_offset: per-station PC accuracy, total propagation
region width centered on the fixed offset. Editable ns/us/ms/s values are accepted.
These are assumptions, not measured audio-device accuracy. Unsupported timing
paths retain the full search and report their limitation.
.TP
.BI \-\-audio\-error " TIME"
Assumed residual audio timing error per station after reported queue compensation;
default 70ms. Default in any timing dropdown resets all four timing controls.
Audio error is total nonnegative lag, zero through the entered value (Default: +70 ms).
It is not a plus/minus allowance; GPS accuracy keeps its existing plus/minus interpretation.
Numeric Audio error edits are independent of GPS accuracy,
propagation and oscillator models. Accepts nonnegative ns/us/ms/s durations up to 60s,
within native profile limits. This does not measure hardware latency or alter
the audio device clock. Live capture requires actual provider timestamps and
uncertainty compatible with the declared calendar and arrival bounds.
.TP
.BI \-\-live\-duplex " yes|no"
Keep hardware capture running during local transmission for physical loopback;
default no. Clear received resets queued input and receiver backlog without
cancelling transmission or clearing its private-key reuse lock.
.TP
.BI \-\-symbol\-burst " off|1|2|3|5|10"
Minimum whole-bit waits between native public DATA slots; default off.
Mandatory pulse support, Helper lead/tail and calendar alignment remain.
Scheduled waits are not scored quiet slots and cannot finish a message.
.TP
.BI \-\-symbol\-burst\-random " 0|1|2|3"
Additional shared-calendar whole-bit waits from zero through this limit;
default 0. Both peers need matching calendar, key/profile and wait settings.
The native keyed permutation has equal counts in each 252-slot cycle.
.TP
.BI \-\-dsss\-factor " 1|10|100|1000"
Added-symbol factor for the native three-carrier waveform; 1 disables added
symbols. Selecting a larger factor in the GUI enables Interleave and adjusts
Rate to preserve the initial occupied bandwidth. Explicit command values retain
their supplied Rate and Carrier. No encryption key is required for this control;
without one, waveform material is publicly reproducible.
.TP
.BI \-\-dsss\-mode " off|interleave"
Select one native symbol lane or interleaved added-symbol lanes. Peers must
share the mode and factor. This does not qualify sensitivity or interference rejection.
.TP
.BI \-\-fhss " off|fake-fcc|fake-eu|fake-list"
Select the canonical FHSS envelope and an illustrative Carrier/Shift schedule.
It does not retune external radio hardware or enable simultaneous reception.
Below 800 MHz both use narrow experimental spacing derived from the waveform;
800 MHz is a product convention, not a regulatory boundary. Higher frequencies
use band-specific profiles or report no applicable profile. The legacy
fake-0.4s-200 identifier maps to fake-fcc. The envelope affects transmitted audio
while the audio IF and hardware tuning remain fixed. Profile details and unimplemented access assumptions
appear under Model limits and references; the illustration is not certification.
.TP
.BI \-\-fhss-list " FREQUENCIES"
For fake-list, give 2–200 distinct Shift frequencies, comma/semicolon separated
or one per line, in Hz/kHz/MHz/GHz/THz (bare values are Hz). Maximum 8192 bytes.
Developer mode exposes Fake: List and the FHSS multiline editor with Apply list.
Zero Shift is allowed. Carrier follows by the same delta, preserving its
difference from Shift. The list illustrates 0.4-second residence, with fixed
audio IF and radio tuning. It has no regulatory profile.
.TP
.BI \-\-fhss-list-file " PATH"
Load a regular text file using the same frequency syntax; # starts an end-of-line
comment. Cannot be combined with --fhss-list. Files are validated before settings
change. Launch and parameter-list exports save the applied frequencies inline,
independent of the source file. Hiding Developer mode retains the active list.
.TP
.BI \-\-search\-margin " N"
Multiply declared frequency and clock accuracy bounds; default 3, minimum 1.
.TP
.BI \-\-target\-snr " DB-HZ"
Set the single Robust target carrier-to-noise-density ratio, from \-200 to
200 dB-Hz. Default: 32. Console and Link Planner edit the same value for all
native per-bit duration planning. It drives a provisional model, not a
sensitivity guarantee, measured in-band SNR or native CPU-capacity forecast.
.TP
.BI \-\-rate " FREQUENCY"
Set the native inner rate, parsed from 0.001 Hz to 30 MHz.
The selected waveform and fixed Helper placement must fit the actual audio rate.
Default: 3.6 kHz.
This is not the payload bit rate.
.B \-\-bw
is an alias.
.TP
.BI \-\-carrier " FREQUENCY"
Set the absolute transmitted/received carrier; default 1725 Hz. Recommendations use the selected
waveform bandwidth and Helper Carrier configuration; valid custom baseband
frequencies are preserved across configuration edits. Carrier minus Shift must
be positive and at most 30 MHz for the real USB stream.
Case-insensitive Hz, kHz, MHz, GHz and THz suffixes, k/m/g/t shorthand, optional
spaces and scientific notation are accepted; a plain number is in Hz.
.B \-\-rf\-carrier
is an alias; matching carrier aliases may be combined.
.TP
.BI \-\-shift " FREQUENCY"
Nonnegative RF translation or LO; default 0 Hz.
Accepts the same frequency units as Carrier.
For Carrier 1.0015 MHz and Shift 1 MHz, the real stream tone is 1500 Hz.
At Shift 0 the same Carrier needs a direct MHz-capable real stream.
.B \-\-rf\-shift
is an alias; choose one shift spelling.
USB is fixed; \-\-sideband upper is a compatibility no-op and lower is rejected.
These settings do not retune hardware or add a complex I/Q interface.
.TP
.BI \-\-dsp\-workspace " auto|PERCENT"
Automatic uses available RAM with bounded host headroom and is the default.
Explicit 25%, 50% or 75% values set a receiver memory limit; the percent sign
may be omitted. Software workers still default to twice effective logical CPU
capacity, with shared in-process ownership and full resource admission.
.PP
Frequencies accept Hz, kHz, MHz, GHz or THz case-insensitively, with optional
spaces, scientific notation or k/m/g/t shorthand; bare numbers mean Hz.
Quote a value containing spaces.
The complete combination must fit the local modem and workspace constraints.
.SS Diagnostics
.TP
.B \-\-self\-check
Run shared GUI and transfer checks, then exit.
No display or audio hardware is required.
.TP
.B \-\-smoke\-test
Run the native GUI workflow checks using simulated audio, then exit.
A working graphical display is required.
.TP
.BI \-\-smoke\-dir " DIRECTORY"
Store smoke-test fixtures in DIRECTORY.
By default, use a temporary directory and remove it after success.
.TP
.BI \-\-smoke\-timeout " SECONDS"
Set the smoke workload allowance, from 10 to 1200 seconds.
Default: 100.
.TP
.BI \-\-smoke\-hold " SECONDS"
Keep the window open after successful smoke checks, from 0 to 60 seconds.
Default: 0.
.TP
.BI \-\-smoke\-view " NAME"
Select the initial page and the page shown after successful smoke checks:
.BR console " (default), " planner ,
.BR compression ,
.BR flow ,
.BR transmission " or " fast-modem .
.TP
.BI \-\-smoke\-scroll " FRACTION"
Set the page scroll position after successful smoke checks, from 0 (top) to
1 (bottom). Default: 0.
.SH OPERATION
Choose an audio device and matching peer settings before transmitting.
Use Message for text or Attach file for an attachment; Use text restores the
text draft. Live audio reception pauses during transmission and resumes afterward.
.PP
In Fast Modem, Ctrl+Enter starts the selected transmit, simulation or loopback
action; Enter inserts a newline.
In Robust Modem, Enter transmits by default; the send preference can require
Ctrl+Enter instead.
Cancel stops the current transmission, simulation or loopback test.
.PP
Robust text of 1\(en16 source bytes uses the native short dictionary. Off sends
its exact short bits; RS20 and RS60 use the configured bounded text-FEC profile.
Longer text uses fixed 1216-slot marker/codeword intervals and the selected FEC,
with raw LZMA2 compression under the native profile. The GUI accepts up to 1 MiB
of decoded text bytes; escaped editor representation has a separate 4 MiB bound.
Long preparation runs in the background on Transmit, with one coded interval
generated at a time. Attachments remain unsupported by Robust.
Explicit raw-bit input accepts 1\(en208 bits and sends them exactly, preserving
leading zeros and partial bytes without text coding or FEC.
.PP
The Robust controller starts with the built-in public keyset
.BR WARNING\-Amateur .
Its key material is included in the program and provides no confidentiality
or sender authentication. It exercises the normal keyed cipher, waveform and
timing paths. A successfully loaded real keyfile replaces it; a failed load
retains the current keys. The Keyfile menu offers
.B WARNING \- Load Amateur
to replace the current bank with the public keyset, and
.B Unload keys
to remove the transmit and receive keys. The normal live transmit guard remains
in effect, but it cannot coordinate independent stations or sessions using the
same key, epoch and stream positions.
.PP
Pending Robust receptions expose bounded bit and native score diagnostics.
Reception completes only after two fully scored quiet public DATA slots.
Interior missing slots remain erasures if DATA resumes; transport EOF,
cancellation, received length and a decodable prefix do not complete a message.
Pending content cannot be copied or saved as a completed message.
Long receptions retain bounded diagnostic prefixes; exact raw-bit copy requires
the full sequence to have been retained. An early short prefix can also begin a
long marker. Missing all later complete uncompressed intervals can leave a valid
shorter stream, even after an underfilled source area. Private-key interval tags
authenticate individual words, not the whole-message endpoint.
.PP
Completed text can be copied or pasted into the message draft.
Received text and suggested filenames use a restricted ASCII presentation;
disallowed source bytes appear as underscores.
Exact completed attachment bytes remain available for an explicit save.
Received files are never opened or executed automatically.
.PP
Developer mode reveals advanced controls and inspection pages.
The Robust Link planner shares the single Target SNR with Console.
Receive-probability and CPU forecasts remain unqualified. Detailed transmission
inspection is deferred separately from the connected live Pattern evidence and
Pattern steps views. Transmit noise uses fresh independent DATA keys; Helper-only
transmission emits no DATA. Force next transmission is a one-shot address-reuse
lock override that retains history and all draft/key/device/resource checks.
Robust uses automatic waveform defaults and the fixed Helper arrangement of
high/low pilot tones and a separate auxiliary tone; these are not selectable.
Its Launch command includes a file-backed keyring's path and named transmit key
via \-\-keyfile, \-\-tx-key named and \-\-key-name (or \-\-tx-key none).
It uses \-\-keyset amateur for the built-in public key and \-\-keyset none for
an unloaded bank. The keyset option cannot be combined with \-\-keyfile.
It contains no key bytes. Reception uses only the selected key, or plaintext
when None is selected. Other loaded ring entries remain available for selection.
Missing files or key names leave the previous settings unchanged.
Its Launch command can be copied for reuse; Load applies validated settings
without executing a shell command or starting a transmission.
.PP
Older copied commands must omit \-\-auto-pattern, \-\-pattern,
\-\-helper-frequency, \-\-short-target-snr and \-\-long-target-snr.
Use \-\-target-snr for the single desired target; the retired options are rejected.
.PP
Click the Robust Console waterfall for a full-window view cropped to the
configured receive search bands. Adjacent columns show amplitude on the left
and wrapped line-to-line phase on the right, relative to nominal FFT-bin
rotation. Black phase is unavailable; noise phase is arbitrary. This view
reuses the environmental FFT and does not improve its native frequency
resolution or certify receiver coverage. Click again or press Escape to return.
Clear waterfall continues to clear history separately.
.SH FILES
Keyfiles and attachment paths are chosen through the interface.
Received content is kept in bounded RAM, with no receive cache or temporary
source files on disk. Save needed content before clearing it or closing the
application; older entries may be evicted at the memory limit.
Saving received files and generating keyfiles do not overwrite existing files.
Keyfile generation collects fresh input from the selected audio device as a
supplement to operating-system randomness. If input is unavailable or unusable,
the completed operation warns that it used only operating-system randomness.
In a browser, enable microphone and audio first.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B DISPLAY
X display used by Linux desktop builds.
.TP
.B ALSA_PLUGIN_DIR
ALSA plugin directory for Linux live audio.
An explicit value takes precedence over portable-bundle host-plugin discovery.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Normal exit, or successful requested check.
This does not assert that every attempted reception succeeded.
.TP
.B 1
Invalid arguments, startup failure or failed check.
.TP
.B 75
The smoke workload allowance expired with recent sampled-transmission progress.
The diagnostic begins with
.BR "INCOMPLETE GUI_SMOKE_BUDGET:" .
Coverage is incomplete; this is not a successful smoke test.
.SH EXAMPLES
Open the desktop console:
.PP
.nf
    datapump-gui
.fi
.PP
Simulate a Robust link with a common 40 dB-Hz target:
.PP
.nf
    datapump-gui \-\-simulation \-\-target\-snr 40 \-\-rate 1.2kHz
.fi
.PP
Check an installation without opening a window:
.PP
.nf
    datapump-gui \-\-self\-check
.fi
.PP
Audio error accepts default or a duration; default means total positive lag
from zero through 70ms. GPS accuracy retains its symmetric per-station bound.
Fake FHSS guards include both endpoint clocks and the propagation region.
Offset delays the nominal receiving hop preview without delaying TX tuning.

.SH SEE ALSO
